# Prettier's own ignore file. Everything not listed here is formatted —
# including `eslint.config.mjs` and `prettier.config.mjs`, which are source like
# any other.
#
# `tsconfig.base.json` is deliberately absent. It is JSONC, and Prettier infers
# the plain `json` parser for it because the `.base.` infix misses Prettier's
# JSONC filename list — but that parser preserves comments regardless, so the
# per-option reasoning in it survives formatting. Verified; no `overrides` entry
# is needed and none should be added.

# Build output.
dist/
build/
coverage/

# Storybook's static build (Task 1.4.5). Named neither `dist` nor `build`, so
# the entries above do not reach it. Mirrored in eslint.config.mjs.
storybook-static/

# The browser suite's failure artefacts (Task 1.13.2). Playwright writes a
# trace zip, a screenshot and a `.last-run.json` here; the JSON is the only
# thing Prettier would have an opinion about, and a formatter rewriting a
# runner's own state file is a diff per run. Mirrored in .gitignore and
# eslint.config.mjs.
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Incremental build state. A single-line JSON blob that TypeScript rewrites on
# every build and Prettier would otherwise expand into a formatted document,
# producing a diff per build.
*.tsbuildinfo

# Generated by pnpm; owned by pnpm.
pnpm-lock.yaml

node_modules/

# Git worktrees — entire second checkouts of this repository, nested inside it.
# Root-level `prettier .` (Task 1.1.7) walks into them otherwise and rewrites
# files belonging to another branch. Mirrored in eslint.config.mjs.
.claude/worktrees/

# Recorded vendor response bodies (Task 2.7.6). Prettier infers a parser for the
# `.html` one and would reformat it — and the entire value of these files is
# that they are BYTE-IDENTICAL to what the vendor sent. A reformatted 401 page
# is a fixture that no longer proves the thing it was recorded to prove, which
# is that a real error body is not JSON.
apps/backend/src/fixtures/alpaca/

# `apps/frontend/src/fixtures/bar-series/` is deliberately NOT listed, and that
# is a decision rather than an omission (Task 2.10.6). Those bodies are recorded
# too, but they are recordings of OUR OWN contract: JSON by construction, and
# what makes them evidence is that the values came off the server rather than out
# of somebody's head — which whitespace cannot change. The entry above exists
# because a vendor's bytes are the claim; ours are not, so they stay in the
# formatter's hands. The reversal trigger is the first fixture of ours whose
# BYTES are the claim: a non-JSON body, or one recorded to prove an encoding.
